# Yildizim

Closed-source 3D galaxy visualization and gamification layer
for knowledge bases. Ships as the differentiator on PIIIX's *hosted*
version of [Chapters](https://github.com/PIIIX-org/chapters) — an
open-core relationship:

- **Self-hosted Chapters** (open source, free) — a plain node-graph
  visualization, in the spirit of Graphify.
- **PIIIX-hosted Chapters** (paid) — same Chapters backend, plus Yildizim's
  proprietary galaxy/gamification visualization on top.

**Status: vision/research phase. No code.** This README is the working
record of the vision as it's been described so far — nothing here is
finalized, and the visual language especially is still being explored.

## Core visual hierarchy

| Chapters concept | Yildizim visual |
|---|---|
| Project | Galaxy |
| Vault (a section of a project) | Star |
| Note | Planet, orbiting its vault-star |
| Sub-note / comment | Moon, orbiting its note-planet |

**Size** of any body (star, planet, moon) is driven by two things together:
how much information it holds, and how much it's actually being used.

## Zoom levels

- Zoomed in: individual planets and moons orbiting a star, visible in
  detail.
- Zoomed out: stars pull together into a single spinning galaxy.
- Zoomed out further: many galaxies visible at once, chained together
  wherever cross-project relationships exist.

## Opening moment

On open, the camera doesn't land on a static overview — it zooms straight
into the single biggest galaxy in the graph, as an animated entry.

## Decay & neglect

An untouched note/star/galaxy doesn't just sit there — it dims, then
fades, then eventually collapses into a cloud of dust. Recovering it back
into a real galaxy/star again requires active work. Decay is a real,
reversible mechanic tied to actual neglect, not a cosmetic dimming effect.

## System activity as visuals

- Data flowing between parts of the system = animated space shuttles.
- An AI performing a search = a visible spaceship moving through the
  scene.

## Dual view modes (for companies/teams)

1. **By project** — the default hierarchical galaxy view described above.
2. **By user** — shows each team member's own knowledge base as its own
   visual shape/size, even when their content isn't connected to the
   shared knowledge base. Privacy-preserving by design: a teammate can see
   that someone has a galaxy of a certain size and shape — visible
   activity, visible scale — but never what's actually inside it. Visual
   only, never content.

## Gamification

- Finishing/completing a project = "conquering a galaxy."
- Keeping a project alive, updated, and versioned earns points.
- Achievements, shareable to social media.
- Explicit goal: keep users engaged and motivated to build, and push back
  against procrastination and neglect — especially in a company/startup
  context, where the by-user view creates social visibility into who's
  actively building and who isn't.
- Must support multiplayer for company/startup use — this isn't a
  single-player mechanic bolted onto a solo tool.
- **Reward system**: still being worked out, but the project-evolution
  ladder below (see "Project evolution") is most of the answer — leveling
  a project through cosmic stages, with a deliberate, shareable milestone
  moment, *is* the core reward loop. What a black-hole project earns
  someone beyond the visual (points, a title, something else) is still
  open.

## Project evolution

A project (galaxy) progresses through visual stages driven by real
activity, grounded in research findings on gamification/decay (see
[`docs/research-3d-gamification-precedents.md`](docs/research-3d-gamification-precedents.md)):

1. **Nebula** — brand new, minimal content. A soft, undefined gas cloud,
   no fixed shape yet.
2. **Red Dwarf** — small but real, consistent activity. Small, dim, warm
   glow.
3. **Main Sequence** — steady, active project. Medium size, clear warm
   white-yellow light. Most healthy projects should live here.
4. **Blue Giant** — highly active, multiple contributors, frequent
   updates. Large, intensely bright.
5. **Supernova** — not a resting stage, a *moment*. Triggered explicitly
   (someone marks a real milestone — a v1.0 ship, a major release) rather
   than happening passively. The celebratory, shareable-to-social-media
   instant.
6. **Black hole** — the permanent aftermath. Visually compact again, but
   it bends light and space around it, visibly pulling nearby stars and
   galaxies toward it. Not bright — *gravitational*. This is what
   "conquered a galaxy" looks like: the project is now significant enough
   to warp everything else in the company's sky toward it.

**Progression (stages 1→4)** is automatic, weighted toward sustained
cadence over time and number of distinct contributors — not raw edit
count, which would let someone game it by dumping a week of work into one
sitting.

**The supernova (4→5→6) is a deliberate action**, not automatic — giving
real agency over a project's biggest achievement moment instead of it
just quietly happening.

**Decay** reverses the same stages for neglected projects below
black-hole tier — dims, shrinks — always with a visible countdown ("cools
from Blue Giant to Main Sequence in 12 days without activity"), never a
surprise. This is the line the research draws between motivating and
manipulative (dark-pattern) design.

At black-hole tier specifically, full reversion to nebula would erase
something genuinely earned, so neglect instead removes the gravitational
pull and dims it toward a dark remnant — still there, still yours, just
no longer pulling focus. Resuming work brings the pull back quickly;
earned status is remembered, not reset from zero.

## Zoom transitions

Flying from the full multi-galaxy sky into one project, then into one
system, the rest of the sky doesn't vanish or stay fully rendered — it
recedes into a dimmed, soft backdrop. This keeps peripheral awareness of
the bigger company picture (relevant to the by-user social-visibility
goal above) while sharpening focus on what's actually being navigated
into.

## Rendering

Full 3D. Fly through, rotate, navigate freely — not a flat 2D "look up at
the sky" view.

## Design process note

UI/UX has been called out as the single most important part of this
project. The next step is real research — existing 3D knowledge-graph
tools, gamification mechanics from products and games that do this well,
rendering/technical approaches — rather than guessing at the details from
scratch.
